+**Do not commit what you did not read, and staging by name is not enough.** A live
+delegate's edits sit in your working tree. `git add -A` is the obvious trap. The quieter
+one: **`git commit` commits the whole index, not the paths you just added.** A delegate
+staging its own work as it goes, which is the right instruction, stages into that same
+index, so your next commit takes its files under a message about something else.
+
+Name the paths at commit time, not only at add time: `git commit -- <paths>` or
+`git commit --only <paths>`. Two catches. A pathspec commit only sees tracked changes, so
+a new file still needs its `git add` and contributes nothing silently without one. And a
+pathspec built from `git diff --cached --name-only` is the whole index dressed as a list,
+so passing it sweeps everything exactly as passing nothing would. Build the pathspec from
+the paths you told the delegate to own, and filter the index against those, never the
+reverse. When a delegate's work does belong in your commit, read it first and say so.
 
